.TH POST9P 2
.SH NAME
post9p \- post a 9P server in the srv device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "post9p.m";
post9p := load Post9p Post9p->PATH;

Post: adt {
	path:	string;
	unpost:	fn(p: self ref Post);
};

init:	fn();
post:	fn(spec: string, conns: chan of ref Sys->FD): (ref Post, string);
postas:	fn(spec, name: string, conns: chan of ref Sys->FD): (ref Post, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B #s
.I spec
is one directory per
.I spec
(and user) across every name space: a file posted there is where any
process of the user finds a server, whatever its name space was built
from, as Plan 9 programs find one in
.BR /srv :
.IP
.EX
mount -A '#seditor/fs' /mnt/editor
.EE
.PP
.B Post
posts a file in
.BI #s spec
named
.BR fs ,
or
.BR fs.1 ,
.BR fs.2 ...
when one is taken (a second instance of the program); it returns the
.B Post
or the reason it could not.
.B Postas
posts it under the caller's
.IR name .
Each open of the posted file is a 9P connection of its own: the server
end of a pipe for it is sent on
.IR conns ,
and the caller serves 9P on it (a
.B Styxserver
each, say, sharing the program's state).
The posted file goes when the program does, or at
.BR unpost ;
connections made through it stay until their clients go.
.PP
A process with
.B NODEVS
cannot attach
.BI #s spec\f1,
so an agent's tools reach a posted server only through a trusted
wrapper that mounts it for them (as
.IR tools9p (8)
does for the editor and Charon).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/post9p.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sys-file2chan (2),
.IR styxservers (2),
.IR editor (1)
